'''alfiona, alfione''' (al-fi-ō′nä̤, al′fi-ōn), ''n.'' [Mex. Sp.] An embiotocoid fish, ''Rhacochilus toxotes'', with small scales, uniserial and jaw teeth, and
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Alfiona (''Rhacochilus toxotes'').}}}}
{{c|{{smaller|(From Report of U. S. Fish Commission, 1884.)}}}}
lip free and deeply cut along its margin. {{smaller|It is the largest as well as the most valuable food-fish of the surf-fish family, ''Embiotocidæ'', and is common along the Californian coast, where it is also called ''sprat'' and ''perch''.}}

'''al fresco''' (äl fres′kō). [It., lit. in the cool air: ''al'' for ''a il'' (< L. ''ad illum''), in the; ''fresco'', cool or fresh air, < ''fresco'', cool, fresh, < OHG. ''frisc'' = E. ''fresh'': see ''fresh'', ''fresco''.] In the open air; out of doors: as, to dine ''al fresco''.
{{smaller block|Much of the gayety and brightness of ''al-fresco'' life.
{{right|''The Century'', XXVII. 190.}}}}
{{smaller block|Such ''al fresco'' suppers the country-gentlemen of Italy ate in the first century of our era! ''D. G. Mitchell'', Wet Days.}}

